  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Sweeping Baltimore in a doubleheader reset the deficit noise, but fragility is still the theme.",
    "what_this_means": "Yesterday's back-to-back wins. 6-3 and 6-3. Should have felt decisive. They weren't. The pattern is consistent: this team plays even baseball until late innings crack it open, then holds on. Both games followed the same shape: tied through five, then runs in the sixth and seventh, then barely survive the ninth. Will Warren and the bullpen reliably converted late leads into saves, but the margin stayed thin both times. Six hits. Twelve strikeouts. A lineup that doesn't produce until the game is already decided. That rhythm works in a doubleheader at home against a team playing out the string. It breaks when the opponent punishes early incompetence or when the bullpen can't find the exit door in a tight ninth. The division is 4.5 back now. Half a game closer than yesterday. But the Yankees are still chasing with three weeks left. These two-run victories are real wins, but they're built on the assumption that late scoring will always arrive. They're winning by not losing early, not by dominating anything. That works until the pitching fails or the bats go quiet in the seventh.",
    "what_to_watch": "Baltimore's .234 average lines up perfectly with Rodr\u00edguez's season 3.69 ERA tomorrow at 3:20 PM. The Orioles have been neutralized all week.",
